1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,031
It is closing time at the Corner Cafe.

2
00:00:03,051 --> 00:00:06,865
Today, Asha wrote every expense on a scrap
of paper.

3
00:00:06,885 --> 00:00:09,150
Milk, coffee beans, paper cups.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,252
Look closely.

5
00:00:10,272 --> 00:00:11,819
Milk appears twice.

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:24,238
Beans, 30.

7
00:00:24,258 --> 00:00:29,087
Milk was bought twice, 12.50 and 8, so
20.50.

8
00:00:29,107 --> 00:00:32,212
Easy on four scraps of paper.

9
00:00:32,232 --> 00:00:33,613
Not on forty!

10
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,400
Welcome to Chapter 15, the third boss
level!

11
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:42,970
You will combine lists, dictionaries, sets
and tuples into one tool that answers that

12
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:44,343
question for any day.

13
00:00:44,367 --> 00:00:48,866
And an AI assistant will sound completely
sure at every stage.

14
00:00:48,886 --> 00:00:51,491
Your tests will decide who is right.

15
00:00:52,083 --> 00:00:53,527
Here is the mission.

16
00:00:53,547 --> 00:00:57,333
Asha types each expense: a category, then
an amount.

17
00:00:57,367 --> 00:01:00,017
When the cafe closes, she types done.

18
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:05,303
The tracker then prints a total for each
category, the total for the day, and the

19
00:01:05,323 --> 00:01:06,407
biggest category.

20
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:09,895
And, like the biscuit jar game, no answer
may crash it.

21
00:01:10,487 --> 00:01:12,382
You know almost every piece.

22
00:01:12,402 --> 00:01:14,297
A while loop from Chapter 7.

23
00:01:14,333 --> 00:01:16,213
A list from Chapter 11.

24
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:18,463
A dictionary from Chapter 12.

25
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:21,110
Sets and tuples from Chapter 13.

26
00:01:21,133 --> 00:01:27,570
And a habit from Chapter 14: run the old
and the new version on the same test

27
00:01:27,590 --> 00:01:28,073
cases.

28
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:32,782
Plus one small new tool: the replace
method with a third value, to check an

29
00:01:32,802 --> 00:01:34,098
amount such as 12.50.

30
00:01:34,683 --> 00:01:36,455
Here is where we are going.

31
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:41,763
On the right, Asha types four expenses,
the same four as on her scraps of paper.

32
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:42,817
She types done.

33
00:01:42,837 --> 00:01:47,520
The tracker prints one row for each
category, in alphabetical order.

34
00:01:47,567 --> 00:01:52,737
Then the number of expenses, the total for
the day, and the biggest category: beans.

35
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:54,682
About twenty-five lines of code.

36
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:58,069
By the end of this video, you will
understand every one.

37
00:01:58,650 --> 00:02:01,990
Asha writes down what done means first.

38
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:02,804
One and two.

39
00:02:02,824 --> 00:02:07,946
It asks until done, and it accepts only
four categories: milk, beans, cups and

40
00:02:07,966 --> 00:02:08,473
repairs.

41
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:08,899
Three.

42
00:02:08,919 --> 00:02:11,626
It accepts amounts such as 30 or 12.50.

43
00:02:11,646 --> 00:02:15,820
A comma, a word or a minus sign gets a
message, not a crash.

44
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:16,771
Four and five.

45
00:02:16,791 --> 00:02:21,326
A total for each category, the day's
total, and the biggest category.

46
00:02:21,346 --> 00:02:23,637
An empty day prints: Biggest, none.

47
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:24,180
And six.

48
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,710
It passes at least five test days with
known results.

49
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,631
Quick check, using criterion five.

50
00:02:30,651 --> 00:02:33,441
Which test day must print: Biggest, none?

51
00:02:44,813 --> 00:02:44,933
A.

52
00:02:44,953 --> 00:02:46,541
A day with no expenses.

53
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:50,806
There is no biggest category, and the
tracker must not crash.

54
00:02:50,826 --> 00:02:52,065
Remember this day.

55
00:02:52,085 --> 00:02:53,113
It will matter.

56
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:54,701
Take a breather.

57
00:02:54,721 --> 00:02:57,140
You know the tool, and what done means.

58
00:02:57,167 --> 00:03:01,366
Next, Asha asks an AI assistant how to
store the data.

59
00:03:06,117 --> 00:03:11,929
In this project, all six AI stages help
build one tracker, piece by piece.

60
00:03:11,949 --> 00:03:13,032
Stage 1: plan.

61
00:03:13,052 --> 00:03:16,421
Asha asks the AI how to store the
expenses.

62
00:03:16,441 --> 00:03:17,367
No code yet.

63
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:23,342
The AI suggests a dictionary: the category
is the key, and the amount is the value.

64
00:03:23,362 --> 00:03:24,420
It sounds tidy.

65
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:27,087
Now remember Asha's scraps of paper.

66
00:03:27,107 --> 00:03:28,481
Milk appears twice.

67
00:03:28,501 --> 00:03:31,929
And remember a rule from Chapter 12 about
keys.

68
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:43,481
No.

69
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:45,839
Keys in a dictionary are unique.

70
00:03:45,859 --> 00:03:47,386
Asha tests it anyway.

71
00:03:47,987 --> 00:03:52,397
Lines 3 to 5 store milk, then beans, then
milk again.

72
00:03:52,433 --> 00:03:55,853
The second milk replaced the first one.

73
00:03:55,873 --> 00:03:57,793
12.50 has disappeared!

74
00:03:57,833 --> 00:04:03,682
This is the AI skill of this chapter: let
AI suggest a data model, then test it

75
00:04:03,702 --> 00:04:05,390
against your real data.

76
00:04:05,987 --> 00:04:08,338
So Asha chooses a different model.

77
00:04:08,358 --> 00:04:11,477
A list of tuples: one tuple for each
expense.

78
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,146
Nothing is ever lost.

79
00:04:13,166 --> 00:04:15,130
Two milks are two tuples.

80
00:04:15,167 --> 00:04:19,171
And at the end, the program builds a
dictionary of totals from the list.

81
00:04:19,750 --> 00:04:20,933
Time to build.

82
00:04:20,953 --> 00:04:21,534
Step 1.

83
00:04:21,554 --> 00:04:23,940
Line 2 starts an empty list.

84
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:26,607
Lines 3 and 4 append one tuple each.

85
00:04:26,627 --> 00:04:30,967
Append takes one value, so each tuple has
its own brackets.

86
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,210
Line 5 prints the list, and line 6 counts
the expenses.

87
00:04:35,233 --> 00:04:40,916
Line 7 unpacks the first tuple into two
variables, as in Chapter 13.

88
00:04:40,936 --> 00:04:43,348
Index 0 is the first expense.

89
00:04:54,713 --> 00:04:55,758
Milk, 12.5.

90
00:04:55,778 --> 00:05:00,793
The first tuple gives category milk and
amount 12.5.

91
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:03,268
And line 6 says 2 expenses.

92
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:04,330
Step 2.

93
00:05:04,350 --> 00:05:07,900
Line 2 keeps the four allowed categories
in a set.

94
00:05:07,933 --> 00:05:10,855
Line 4 starts a loop that runs until
break.

95
00:05:10,875 --> 00:05:14,892
Line 5 asks for a category, and calls two
methods in a row.

96
00:05:14,912 --> 00:05:18,723
Strip removes the spaces, and lower makes
small letters.

97
00:05:18,767 --> 00:05:21,937
Lines 6 and 7: done stops the loop.

98
00:05:21,967 --> 00:05:26,337
Lines 8 and 9: any answer that is not in
the set gets a message.

99
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:30,204
Otherwise, lines 11 and 12 count it and
confirm it.

100
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:31,848
Stage 2: write.

101
00:05:31,868 --> 00:05:33,874
Asha wrote this loop herself.

102
00:05:33,894 --> 00:05:38,694
Before she runs it, she asks the AI what
it prints for three answers.

103
00:05:38,714 --> 00:05:40,720
Make your own prediction too.

104
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:52,342
Yes.

105
00:05:52,362 --> 00:05:57,268
The AI agrees: strip and lower turn it
into milk, which is in the set.

106
00:05:57,288 --> 00:06:00,506
Tea gets the message, and done stops the
loop.

107
00:06:01,083 --> 00:06:01,880
Asha runs it.

108
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:04,393
Milk with a space is accepted: OK, milk.

109
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:06,973
Tea gets the message.

110
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,633
And done stops the loop, with one category
entered.

111
00:06:10,653 --> 00:06:13,570
The AI's prediction matches the real run.

112
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:15,790
Step 3: the amount.

113
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:19,895
In Chapter 10, is digit checked a whole
number.

114
00:06:19,915 --> 00:06:25,310
But 12.50 has a decimal point, and is
digit says False for it.

115
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:29,768
The fix: remove one decimal point first,
then check.

116
00:06:29,788 --> 00:06:35,251
Replace takes an optional third value: how
many changes to make.

117
00:06:35,271 --> 00:06:39,363
Line 2 removes one point, so 12.50 becomes
1250.

118
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:44,225
Line 3 removes only the first point of
1.2.3, so it becomes 12.3.

119
00:06:44,245 --> 00:06:45,940
That still has a point.

120
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:53,319
Line 4 loops over six texts, and line 5
prints each one with the result of the

121
00:06:53,339 --> 00:06:53,886
check.

122
00:07:05,247 --> 00:07:07,294
Only two: 12.50 and 30.

123
00:07:07,314 --> 00:07:12,507
A comma, a word, a second point and a
minus sign all fail.

124
00:07:12,533 --> 00:07:17,590
Only text with at most one point and
nothing but digits passes.

125
00:07:17,610 --> 00:07:19,121
Then float is safe.

126
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:21,864
Quick check, with four new answers.

127
00:07:21,884 --> 00:07:23,967
Which one passes the amount check?

128
00:07:35,347 --> 00:07:36,124
C, 0.75.

129
00:07:36,144 --> 00:07:40,809
Without its one point, it is 0 7 5: all
digits.

130
00:07:40,829 --> 00:07:46,093
And 1, dot, dot, 5 keeps a second point,
so it fails.

131
00:07:46,687 --> 00:07:47,276
Breather.

132
00:07:47,296 --> 00:07:48,426
Relax your hands.

133
00:07:48,446 --> 00:07:51,337
The tracker can now collect clean
expenses.

134
00:07:51,367 --> 00:07:52,985
Next, the totals.

135
00:07:57,717 --> 00:07:58,238
Step 4.

136
00:07:58,258 --> 00:08:01,795
Line 2 is the list of tuples, with milk
twice.

137
00:08:01,815 --> 00:08:05,197
Line 3 starts an empty dictionary of
totals.

138
00:08:05,233 --> 00:08:08,853
Line 4 unpacks each tuple into category
and amount.

139
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:10,768
Line 5 is the key line.

140
00:08:10,788 --> 00:08:16,186
Get gives the total so far, or 0 for a
category with no total yet.

141
00:08:16,206 --> 00:08:18,156
Then it adds the amount.

142
00:08:29,513 --> 00:08:29,973
20.5.

143
00:08:29,993 --> 00:08:36,877
The first milk found no total, so get gave
0, and the total became 12.5.

144
00:08:36,897 --> 00:08:39,178
The second milk added 8.

145
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:41,511
Step 5 prints the table.

146
00:08:41,531 --> 00:08:46,260
Line 4 loops over sorted totals: the keys
in alphabetical order.

147
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:48,651
Line 5 is Chapter 3 formatting.

148
00:08:48,671 --> 00:08:51,939
Less than 8 fills the name to 8
characters.

149
00:08:51,959 --> 00:08:57,980
Greater than 7 point 2 f puts the amount
on the right, with two decimal places.

150
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,369
Line 6 prints the count of expenses, and
the sum of the values: 56.75.

151
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:06,877
Step 6: the biggest category.

152
00:09:06,897 --> 00:09:08,085
Stage 3: generate.

153
00:09:08,105 --> 00:09:11,710
Asha asks the AI for one line, and asks
what it gives.

154
00:09:11,733 --> 00:09:14,785
The AI writes biggest equals max of
totals.

155
00:09:14,805 --> 00:09:18,428
It says this gives the category that cost
the most.

156
00:09:18,448 --> 00:09:23,643
In Chapter 11, max found the largest
number in a list, so it looks right.

157
00:09:23,667 --> 00:09:28,791
But remember what a loop over a dictionary
gives you, from Chapter 12.

158
00:09:40,147 --> 00:09:40,339
No.

159
00:09:40,359 --> 00:09:46,001
Max goes through a dictionary like a loop
does, so it compares the keys, not the

160
00:09:46,021 --> 00:09:46,496
totals.

161
00:09:46,516 --> 00:09:47,487
Asha tests it.

162
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:50,011
Beans cost 30, the most.

163
00:09:50,031 --> 00:09:52,037
But the line prints milk!

164
00:09:52,067 --> 00:09:53,705
Max compared the names.

165
00:09:53,725 --> 00:09:56,518
In alphabetical order, milk comes last.

166
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:58,229
Stage 4: test.

167
00:09:58,249 --> 00:10:03,390
Asha asks the AI for test days, each with
the answer it expects.

168
00:10:03,433 --> 00:10:09,291
The AI lists three days: three categories,
one category, and a day where repairs cost

169
00:10:09,311 --> 00:10:09,913
the most.

170
00:10:09,933 --> 00:10:11,427
But it forgot a day.

171
00:10:11,447 --> 00:10:16,195
Asha remembers criterion five, and adds a
day with no expenses.

172
00:10:16,787 --> 00:10:20,388
Lines 2 to 4 hold the AI's three test
days.

173
00:10:20,408 --> 00:10:24,010
Lines 5 to 7 run the AI's line on each
one.

174
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:36,541
Days 2 and 3.

175
00:10:36,561 --> 00:10:38,540
Day 1 fails with milk.

176
00:10:38,567 --> 00:10:44,229
But Day 3 passed only by luck: repairs is
also last in alphabetical order.

177
00:10:44,249 --> 00:10:46,610
One passing test proves little.

178
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:50,153
Now Asha's own day 4: no expenses.

179
00:10:50,173 --> 00:10:54,670
Line 2 is an empty dictionary, and line 4
calls max.

180
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:59,473
The program prints Summary, then stops
with a Value Error on line 4.

181
00:11:00,087 --> 00:11:02,153
Here are Asha's results.

182
00:11:02,173 --> 00:11:03,197
Day 1 fails.

183
00:11:03,233 --> 00:11:04,633
Day 2 passes.

184
00:11:04,667 --> 00:11:07,417
Day 3 passes, but only by luck.

185
00:11:07,467 --> 00:11:11,857
And Day 4, the day the AI forgot, stops
the program with an error.

186
00:11:12,453 --> 00:11:13,537
Stage 5: debug.

187
00:11:13,557 --> 00:11:17,069
Asha pastes her lines and the full error
report.

188
00:11:17,089 --> 00:11:19,203
She asks for the cause first.

189
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:24,938
The AI explains: an empty dictionary has
no keys, so max has nothing to choose

190
00:11:24,958 --> 00:11:25,304
from.

191
00:11:25,324 --> 00:11:28,313
And it agrees that max compares the keys.

192
00:11:28,333 --> 00:11:33,764
Its fix: start biggest at the text none,
and keep the biggest category so far in a

193
00:11:33,784 --> 00:11:34,096
loop.

194
00:11:34,687 --> 00:11:36,891
Asha does not trust the fix yet.

195
00:11:36,911 --> 00:11:39,255
She runs all four test days again.

196
00:11:39,275 --> 00:11:43,357
Lines 2 to 5 hold the four days, and the
last one is empty.

197
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:45,502
Line 7 starts biggest at none.

198
00:11:45,522 --> 00:11:47,977
Lines 8 to 10 check every category.

199
00:11:47,997 --> 00:11:53,917
A category wins if nothing is chosen yet,
or if its total is bigger than the best so

200
00:11:53,937 --> 00:11:54,200
far.

201
00:11:54,233 --> 00:11:59,331
Here are the results: beans, cups,
repairs, and none for the empty day.

202
00:11:59,351 --> 00:12:00,700
All four days pass!

203
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:01,996
Quick check.

204
00:12:02,016 --> 00:12:05,012
What does max compare when you give it a
dictionary?

205
00:12:16,380 --> 00:12:16,507
B.

206
00:12:16,527 --> 00:12:17,170
The keys.

207
00:12:17,190 --> 00:12:21,146
A dictionary gives its keys, like a loop
over it does.

208
00:12:21,753 --> 00:12:22,326
Breather.

209
00:12:22,346 --> 00:12:24,629
The AI was wrong, and sounded sure.

210
00:12:24,649 --> 00:12:26,603
Different test days caught it.

211
00:12:26,633 --> 00:12:28,399
Now, the complete tracker.

212
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:36,118
Step 7: the complete tracker, in two
halves.

213
00:12:36,138 --> 00:12:39,920
Lines 2 and 3: the set of categories, and
an empty list.

214
00:12:39,967 --> 00:12:42,759
Lines 4 to 9 are the loop from step 2.

215
00:12:42,779 --> 00:12:45,497
On the right, Asha's four categories.

216
00:12:45,533 --> 00:12:50,007
Lines 11 to 15 ask for the amount and
check it, as in step 3.

217
00:12:50,027 --> 00:12:53,100
A good amount becomes a tuple in the list.

218
00:12:53,720 --> 00:12:55,086
The second half.

219
00:12:55,106 --> 00:12:59,070
Lines 16 to 18 build the totals, as in
step 4.

220
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:02,320
The loop on lines 20 to 23 does two jobs.

221
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:07,140
It prints each row, and it keeps the
biggest category so far.

222
00:13:07,167 --> 00:13:10,504
And lines 24 and 25 print the summary.

223
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:11,987
Your turn.

224
00:13:12,007 --> 00:13:15,534
Asha buys paper cups twice: 2, then 3.5.

225
00:13:15,554 --> 00:13:17,308
Then she types done.

226
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:29,739
Expenses, 2.

227
00:13:29,759 --> 00:13:30,817
Total, 5.50.

228
00:13:30,837 --> 00:13:37,019
Both cups went into the list, and the
totals added them into one row.

229
00:13:37,617 --> 00:13:39,485
Criterion six says: test it.

230
00:13:39,505 --> 00:13:44,745
Each test is a copy of the tracker, with
its own typed answers and an expected

231
00:13:44,765 --> 00:13:45,217
result.

232
00:13:45,267 --> 00:13:48,017
A normal day, the day you saw at the
start.

233
00:13:48,067 --> 00:13:49,397
An empty day.

234
00:13:49,433 --> 00:13:51,983
Bad amounts, with a comma and a word.

235
00:13:52,033 --> 00:13:53,643
Unknown categories.

236
00:13:53,667 --> 00:13:54,902
And one expense.

237
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:57,242
The bad amounts test.

238
00:13:57,262 --> 00:14:03,878
Asha types milk with 12 comma 50, then
milk with the word twelve, then milk with

239
00:14:03,898 --> 00:14:04,375
12.50.

240
00:14:04,395 --> 00:14:06,615
Line 12 checks each amount.

241
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:18,317
One.

242
00:14:18,337 --> 00:14:22,786
Each bad amount got a message and was
never added.

243
00:14:22,806 --> 00:14:24,484
Only 12.50 counted.

244
00:14:24,504 --> 00:14:24,931
Pass!

245
00:14:25,553 --> 00:14:30,205
The empty day: Total, 0.00, and Biggest,
none.

246
00:14:30,225 --> 00:14:31,119
No crash.

247
00:14:31,139 --> 00:14:31,626
Pass!

248
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:33,601
Unknown categories.

249
00:14:33,621 --> 00:14:36,107
Tea and cake each get the message.

250
00:14:36,127 --> 00:14:40,235
Repairs, with spaces around it, is cleaned
and accepted.

251
00:14:40,255 --> 00:14:40,604
Pass!

252
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:45,629
One expense: cups, 9.00, and Biggest,
cups.

253
00:14:45,649 --> 00:14:46,144
Pass!

254
00:14:46,753 --> 00:14:49,513
The normal day matched at the start of
this video.

255
00:14:49,533 --> 00:14:52,700
And the other four runs you saw match too.

256
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:54,520
All five test days pass.

257
00:14:55,117 --> 00:14:56,391
Stage 6: improve.

258
00:14:56,411 --> 00:14:57,762
Every test passes.

259
00:14:57,782 --> 00:15:04,005
Asha asks for one way to make lines 16 to
18 shorter, and says: do not change what

260
00:15:04,025 --> 00:15:04,767
it prints.

261
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:08,921
The AI suggests plus equals on line 18,
without get.

262
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:11,390
It sounds shorter and sensible.

263
00:15:11,433 --> 00:15:13,701
Remember why line 18 uses get.

264
00:15:13,721 --> 00:15:16,904
Plus equals must read the old total first.

265
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:32,236
There is no milk total yet, so Python
cannot read it.

266
00:15:32,256 --> 00:15:34,636
Asha tests it on three expenses.

267
00:15:35,253 --> 00:15:37,106
Line 5 uses plus equals.

268
00:15:37,126 --> 00:15:42,023
The first milk has no total, and Python
stops with a Key Error.

269
00:15:42,067 --> 00:15:47,755
The shorter line breaks the program, so
Asha keeps get, with its default of 0.

270
00:15:48,353 --> 00:15:51,053
Here are all six stages on one tracker.

271
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:55,660
The AI helped at every stage, from the
data model to the review.

272
00:15:55,700 --> 00:16:00,833
And it was confidently wrong three times:
the model, the biggest category, and the

273
00:16:00,853 --> 00:16:01,650
shorter line.

274
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:05,146
Every time, a test with a known result
caught it.

275
00:16:05,753 --> 00:16:06,663
Last breather.

276
00:16:06,683 --> 00:16:10,313
You built a real data tool, and you made
the decisions.

277
00:16:10,333 --> 00:16:11,365
Let's recap.

278
00:16:16,117 --> 00:16:18,347
Here is what you used in this boss level.

279
00:16:18,367 --> 00:16:22,167
Keep each record as a tuple in a list, so
nothing is lost.

280
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:24,857
Build totals with get and a default of 0.

281
00:16:24,877 --> 00:16:27,860
A set of allowed values rejects anything
else.

282
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:32,980
Replace with a third value, and is digit,
check an amount before float converts it.

283
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,375
Max on a dictionary compares the keys.

284
00:16:35,395 --> 00:16:39,220
To find the biggest total, keep the
biggest so far in a loop.

285
00:16:39,267 --> 00:16:42,589
And test several days, including an empty
one.

286
00:16:42,609 --> 00:16:44,478
One test can pass by luck.

287
00:16:45,087 --> 00:16:46,358
One last check.

288
00:16:46,378 --> 00:16:51,351
Asha types Beans with a capital B, then
the amount 12.5.0.

289
00:16:51,371 --> 00:16:54,192
What does the tracker print next?

290
00:17:05,547 --> 00:17:05,684
D.

291
00:17:05,704 --> 00:17:08,507
Lower makes beans, which is allowed.

292
00:17:08,527 --> 00:17:14,781
But 12.5.0 keeps a second point after one
is removed, so the amount check fails,

293
00:17:14,801 --> 00:17:16,977
with a message, not a crash.

294
00:17:17,587 --> 00:17:19,136
Boss level complete!

295
00:17:19,156 --> 00:17:22,668
You earned 300 XP and the Money Mapper
badge.

296
00:17:22,688 --> 00:17:24,316
And Module 3 is done.

297
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:30,940
The practice file has eight more tasks,
worth 170 XP.

298
00:17:30,967 --> 00:17:35,277
Three warm-ups, worth 10 XP each, such as
checking an amount.

299
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:39,910
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including the smallest category.

300
00:17:39,933 --> 00:17:42,387
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

301
00:17:42,407 --> 00:17:46,733
In the last one, you fix an AI's tracker
that forgets its totals.

302
00:17:46,767 --> 00:17:50,041
Write down the expected result before you
run each one.

303
00:17:50,617 --> 00:17:51,996
Asha's tracker works.

304
00:17:52,016 --> 00:17:57,058
But the biggest-category loop appears in
her tests, her fix and her tracker.

305
00:17:57,078 --> 00:17:59,057
Three copies of the same code.

306
00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:04,426
In Chapter 16, you will learn functions:
named blocks of code that you write once

307
00:18:04,446 --> 00:18:05,680
and use many times.

308
00:18:05,700 --> 00:18:07,355
Do the warm-up tasks now.

309
00:18:07,375 --> 00:18:09,230
They take about ten minutes.

310
00:18:09,267 --> 00:18:11,117
See you in Chapter 16.
